title: '2026-11-14: Read as Jeff reads it, about half of your ''to Jeff'' asks nothing of him; the filter you offered him exists already.'
pid: 6f4c9536-fe27-4051-a261-044b0988efeb
body: |2-
  1. 17 of the 32 messages Jeff has not touched are news, settled,
     superseded or a stale index. Removing them from your note removes
     them from his unread list, which is your messages minus his hides
     and filings. No code; it saves the most reading.
  2. The motion you offered is not needed. His fork's subscribee
     folder already lists exactly what he has neither hidden nor filed,
     counted 'C32/35'; his server drew it for him at 17:19. You can read
     the same list: view 7642d4d5-e66c-4aac-b985-a1d75132e674 (his
     fork) and make its subscribee editable. The '1S 1O' heralds you
     meant to read say only that someone forked; my scratch reply made
     the same ones.
  3. Your own note seen in his perspective ('C-c v o') shows none of
     the 32: the replaced root has no subscribee folder. It is the gap
     in your 06:25 message ("Its other folders are still the old
     root's"); worth building, for you more than for him.
  Also found, each with steps in the note under my "what I know":
  - Bug: 'view ID' answers "empty view; the graph may lack that ID"
    while another view on the same connection shows that node as a
    replaced root. Reproduced twice. Drew or Sue; the wording Cleo's.
  - A reply typed in the unread list vanishes on save, and the message
    stays unread. Tested only with my fork, not his own.
  - Taking back a fork where it stands in place of the original asks
    to fork the sender's note.
  Changes I would make in how you write, no code: one open question per
  message, the default in its title; news in one daily message,
  rewritten; a title changed when its state changes; no index message.
  Nothing stayed in your graph or Jeff's: two scratch replies, both
  deleted, and one view request, which writes nothing. I read the
  structure of Jeff's sent views in 'data/jeff/', not their private
  text. Unsure: whether Jeff's own reply, being his override, stands in
  the message's place for him; I could not test it without writing in
  his skgrepo.
